WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
*screams*

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:02.800
*grunts*

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.740
"Sweet Christmas."

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.920
Way to neglect the Jewish viewers, Luke!

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.880
Sweet Hanukkah much?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.760
*"Shaft' rip-off*

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.360
*whack*

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.060
*Film Theory theme*

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.540
Hello, Internet!  Welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.140
the Cross-Fit for your brain!  Can I be honest?

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.060
When I first heard about Cross-Fit, I thought it was, like, a cooperative board game or something.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.540
All right, all right, let's talk about Marvel and Netflix.  We all love Marvel movies, right?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:54.540
As evidenced by all the requests I get from you, that is a pretty solid "Yes."  And 2017 is going to be an awesome year for them.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.580
"Guardians 2" and "Spider-Man"?  I cannot wait!

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.380
But over the holidays, I took some time to catch up on the REST of the Marvel universe,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.720
you know, the guys who are still waiting for their crack at the big screen.  And without question, the biggest of those guys is Luke Cage.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.180
Now, over here on Film Theory, I don't know if you've noticed it, but I seem to have an obsession with trying to kill superheroes.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.940
(echoing voice) ". . . can be brought down by a simple bullet."

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.820
"Destroy the brain, and Wade Wilson, as we know him, ceases to be."

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.560
"Once lured in, Superman won't know what hit him."

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.520
Oh man, looking at that, if I wasn't me and I saw that, I would be very concerned about my mental state,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:34.960
but luckily, I AM me, and all murderous-sociopathy aside, maybe it's just my long-held desire to be a super-villain,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.820
or, quite simply, maybe it's my desire to be able to beat brawn with brains,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.080
or maybe I just want some compelling but ultimately honest click-bait.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.560
I am not really sure, probably some combination of all three of those things.  What I AM sure about those

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.660
is that one superhero is safe, and that one is Aquaman because your powers suck.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:57.400
Way to put innocent marine life into the line of fire for ya!  But enough about Aquaman because Sweet, Sweet Christmas,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.160
Luke Cage, today, it is you who has drawn the short straw.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.000
In other words, it is time for you to meet my Lucille.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.280
Well, it's actually a metaphorical Lucille because literally hitting Luke with a barbed-wire

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.800
wouldn't have any effect due to his ONE superpower--

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:17.560
That's right, throughout Marvel's "Jessica Jones" and "Luke Cage," Luke is routinely shot, stabbed, blown up,

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.960
shot again, stabbed again, and blown up again, but all of it has no effect on Luke

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.980
He's like a steel, electric-type Pokemon.  He just walks away without a scratch.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.540
Daredevil was bedridden half of season 2 because of a couple of punches,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.980
and Jessica Jones received her fair share of scars, but not Luke!

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:40.120
The villains of the series, Cottonmouth and Diamondback, have to literally resort to alien bullets just to have a fighting chance against the guy.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:45.060
So this got me to thinking, "Is there a simpler solution here than just magic ET bullets?"

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.180
What if everything you needed was right here on Earth at your very fingertips?

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.160
Well, get ready, Luke, 'cause the rise of Dr. Theory is upon you!

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.900
*evil laugh, thunderclap*

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.280
Hey, I said I wanted to be a super-villain, OK; I didn't say I had a good name picked out yet.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.980
EVIL MIND!  Nope, no. ANTIPAT!  No . . . nothing, never mind.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.280
So, like I said, ultimately all of Luke Cage's powers boil down to one thing--his skin,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:16.060
skin made out of three layers measuring 2 millimeters thick and creating one impenetrable body,

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.440
and that's it!  Get past that outer-Wall Sina and he's just as vulnerable as you or me.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.000
As we learn about at the end of "Jessica Jones," after Luke takes a shotgun to the face(!),

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.980
the whiplash from the shotgun blast causes Luke's brain to slam against his skull putting him into an immediate coma.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.620
So to take Luke Cage out, we just need to get to those squishy innards of his.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.600
Now, throughout season one of this Netflix series,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.680
every single villain has focused on piercing Luke's 2 millimeter fortress of protection

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.340
via knives, guns, rocket launchers, shotguns, alien bullets,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.800
but everyone's been looking at this from the wrong perspective.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.140
The focus shouldn't be on breaking THROUGH Luke's skin but instead lowering his defenses from the inside out,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.040
turning his enhanced skin back into what it once was--

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.300
regular, old, easy-to-pierce, please-don't-look-at-my-saggy-love-handles, human skin.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.900
And in order to do that, we need to look at his origin story.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:08.640
Now, before Luke Cage was a superhuman with the toughest skin on earth,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.660
he was just your average guy with normal skin and HORRIFIC FACIAL HAIR!

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:17.000
Man, Marvel, I know your budgets aren't quite the same for the Netflix series,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.400
but geez!  It looks like he glued a Chia pet onto his chin!

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.740
Anyway, while in prison, Luke Cage is beaten nearly to death after an altercation with other inmates.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:32.280
He's only saved when the prison doctor, Dr. Burstein (sp?), performs an experimental treatment on him, resulting in his impenetrable skin.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.680
Later on in the series, Dr. Burstein actually reveals the source of Luke's superhuman abilities--

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:43.020
Luke's chemical composition, his DNA, has been fused with the foreign DNA of an abalone.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.000
"We used a process called CRISPR to fuse the subject's DNA with another DNA to gain its attributes."

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.180
Now, believe it or not, but this CRISPR process that Dr. Burstein mentions is actually a real thing.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.420
CRISPR stands for--

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.000
It's a very real process wherein specific stretches of genetic code target and edit DNA.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.600
Using the enzyme CAS9, scientists cut out specific strands of DNA and replace it with whatever the heck they want.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.960
It's basically just like what happens in "Jurassic Park" except real, in real life.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.080
The end result is that scientists can modify and enhance genes in cells to make them better.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:27.100
In China, researchers have successfully used CRISPR to create extra-muscular dogs for hunting and military purposes.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.160
---- YOU, CHINA, AND YOUR MUTANT DOGS!

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.980
And what has the U.S. done with this amazing scientific innovation?

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.780
Well, we gave Luke Cage the power of delicious seafood.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.640
"Foreign DNA was abalone shell, wasn't it?"

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:38.660
Yes, an abalone.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.760
It is a snail that lives in the sea, a sea-snail.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.080
Now, I make jokes 'cause that seems lame, but abalone are actually known for their incredibly-hard shells--

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:53.260
The shells are so strong, in fact, that students in UC San Diego are now using them to create body armor,

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:56.620
tougher and stronger than Kevlar!  But it doesn't stop there.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.400
Abalone is just as strong as steel and can even stop bullets!

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.000
So maybe Aquaman's powers aren't so silly after all!

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.140
Imagine him having a team of impenetrable shellfish as bodyguards!

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.300
All right, on second thought, it's still really dumb!

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:14.900
Speaking of dumb, Aquaman's hair.  Go get some conditioner because the sea salt is really starting to build up there, buddy.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.140
So one method we see in the show is eroding what makes the abalone so strong.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:25.260
Dr. Burstein implements this tactic when trying to remove the alien shrapnel from Luke's body in a series of acid baths.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.680
Per Dr. Burstein, abalone on its own is nearly impenetrable, a shield of calcium carbonate plates,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.800
but by destroying the hydrogen covalent bonds between those plates, he can actually break through.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.800
Now, before we go any further, for those  unfamiliar,

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:51.000
a covalent bond is a super-strong chemical bond that pairs electrons between atoms ,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.400
strengthening their outer shells and stabilizing them.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.880
Basically, think of a covalent bond like a marriage.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.480
(sultry voice) Two hot, single, young atoms catch each others' eyes from across the molecular field,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.920
recognize that they need the other one to fill a hole in their life, or at least their valence shell,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.560
and a few rounds of Netflix and chill later, they've formed a tight bond--

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.700
a tight, COVALENT bond.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:15.480
(regular voice) Now, in order to perform surgery on Luke Cage, Dr. Burstein needs to break those covalent bonds.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.740
Dr. Burstein is basically the hussy from across the street trying to ---- in on your man!

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:24.260
(feminine voice) Homewrecker!  After you gave your atomic energy to that no-good cheatin' husband of yours!

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.420
(regular voice) It's like an episode of nerdy "Jerry Springer."

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.520
To break up the bonds, the doctor recreates the exact same circumstances that caused Luke's powers in the first place,

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.780
and, scientifically speaking, this is completely the right approach.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:40.020
The only way to break a covalent bond is by recreating what caused the bond in the first place.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.320
So way to go "Luke Cage" writers!  You have done your homework!

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.940
(judgmental tone) Unlike others.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:45.440
*crash!*

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:54.400
So to break those covalent bonds that bind you, you have to match the same amount of energy needed to form the bond in the first place.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.400
For Luke Cage, Dr. Burstein knows the reactants that formed Luke's super-skin--

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.380
acid, saline, Luke's DNA, and abalone DNA--

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:06.380
but he doesn't know the amount of energy needed to break up that product.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.540
The doctor says as much in episode nine.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.080
Quite simply, Burstein is unsure just how strong the covalent bonds are in Luke's body

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:20.540
and dips him into higher and higher temperature of acid bath to find the level where there is enough energy

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:24.580
to break through Luke's skin but not so much energy that it dissolves him alive.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.420
You know, that might be bad.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:30.260
Now, obviously, this is RIDICULOUSLY complicated,

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.800
but I gotta tell ya, there's a much easier way to defeat Luke Cage,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.800
one that doesn't require acid or the existence of aliens with killer ballistics.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:46.120
Given the controversy surrounding human genome splicing, geneticists have created ways to reverse the CRISPR process.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.360
You know, in case all this genetic meddling leads to some sort of less-than-desired effect,

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.380
like a three-eyed fish or someone who looks like Red Skull or something.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.940
So the true secret to beating Luke Cage is to reverse the CRISPR process,

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:02.900
but that's gotta be really hard, right?  Well, you'd be surprised.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.680
So scientists typically reverse these effects using what's known as

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.380
which are kind of like super-selective scissors for DNA,

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:18.400
except instead of cutting paper and forming origami flowers, you're cutting into human DNA and forming, uh, superhuman flowers.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:24.000
Each restriction enzyme recognizes a particular DNA sequence and cuts out this sequence within the DNA.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.880
Thus, to reverse Luke Cage's powers, all you have to do is cut out the foreign DNA, that abalone nacre sequence,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:36.780
from the source DNA, Luke Cage, turning him back into a mere, thin-skinned mortal.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:42.220
So at this point you might be saying to yourself, "Well, Matt, that doesn't sound a whole lot easier than dipping him into an acid bath.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.600
Where do I get my hands on restriction enzymes for abalone nacre?"

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.480
To which I say to you, "Google."

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.700
*typing*

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:56.340
And it's only $58!  It's like Black Friday all over again!

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.340
"But then, how do you get all of this into him?" I hear you asking.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.120
"We can't even cut into his skin, so what chance does a needle have?"

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:07.100
To which I say to you, "None!  No chance in heck!  A needle is not the way to go!"

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:13.380
Besides, if you simple injected the restriction enzyme into Luke Cage, his immune system would immediately destroy those foreign proteins (burps)

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.360
and Luke Cage would probably destroy YOU for trying to stab him with a mysterious needle.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:23.600
However, I thought of that, and scientists have already discovered ways to break through the human body's natural barrier system,

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.800
and the answer is--viruses.  Through a process known as--

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.620
scientists package foreign DNA into a virus and then expose it to a cell.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:40.680
Once the virus binds to the living cell, it injects the foreign DNA into the host and the replicates this DNA throughout the body.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:47.240
This of the process like a Trojan horse where a bunch of heavily-armed soldiers sneak their way past your cell's defensive gates.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:51.200
But I know already what you're thinking, "How do you get your hands on a virus?

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.800
I mean, it's not like you can just go online and purchase the common cold off the Internet!"

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:56.440
*typing*

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.800
Yep, you too can have your very own common cold virus

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.940
Get your entire a sick day by releasing viruses throughout your classroom,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.120
just a couple bucks off of Amazon Prime.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:11.860
So with your ingredients in hand,  do a pen-pineapple-apple-pen.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:14.540
I have an enzyme!  I have a virus!

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:15.760
*grunts*

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:16.960
Virus enzyme!

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.840
All you have to do is splice the two together, and *grunt*

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:24.860
Voila!  You have the perfect cocktail to defeat Luke Cage.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:28.660
And the best thing about this virus is that it's airborne.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.200
You don't even have to attempt to pierce Luke Cage's skin,

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.820
just invite him out to the local Java Hut, get two cups of coffee, laugh over what a terrible lawyer Matt Murdoch is,

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:43.160
and then finally wait for the sign that the airborne cold virus you exposed him to in the cafe has taken effect,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.680
turning his impenetrable skin back to normal.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:50.940
Just wait for that first sniffle and BAM!  Another superhero's head to mount on your wall.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.560
It's a heck of a lot better prize than Aquaman.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.060
And with that--

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.860
*evil laugh*

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.200
But hey, that's just a theory--a Film Theory!

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.280
Aaaaaaand cut!

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:13.520
Also, a very special thank-you to the team over at the Wisecrack channel for helping me with the research for this episode.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:18.260
If you enjoyed it, then please do me a favor, and also yourself a favor, and go check them out.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:22.160
They're some of my best friends on YouTube, and where we do mostly science and math-based theories,

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:27.120
they do theories and analyses based on the humanities--philosophy, ethics, [literature], things like that.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:32.880
So if you enjoyed this episode, then you are going to love their analysis on my favorite current animated series "Rick and Morty,"

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:41.180
legitimately one of the funniest, smartest shows on right now, and their analysis of it is one of the funniest, smartest videos of that show online.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:47.560
So pull a Luke Cage and punch that button you see on screen to show them some love and find yourself a new favorite channel.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.880
And make sure you stay tuned.  We have a lot more Marvel on the way next month,

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:55.040
so headbutt that subscribe button to join the notification squad.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.020
(sing-songy voice) Notification Squad!

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:58.920
(regular voice) Gotta go!  See you next week!

